title: "Periodicity Mathematics: A Unified Framework of Epicycle Series and Interdisciplinary Periodic Structures"
abstract: "This work develops a unified mathematical framework for periodic phenomena, based on the principle 'symmetry -> invariant -> closed orbit -> period'. It argues that periodic phenomena across disciplines can be captured by a single mathematical object, a family of epicycles, with relations between periods described by periodic convolution. Periodicity is thereby reduced to a question of group actions and group representations. The methodology rests on four pillars: group representation theory, periodic convolution, window functions and refraction-index diagnostics, and complex exponential activation functions (CEAF). The framework is applied domain by domain in more than twenty fields, from astronomy, physics, biology and psychology to economics, cryptography and computer science."
